## 1. Brute Force Solution

Python

Run

```
class Solution:
    def lexiString(self, s: str) -> str:
        n = len(s)
        ans = s

        for i in range(1, n):
            rotation = s[i:] + s[:i]

            if rotation < ans:
                ans = rotation

        return ans
```

TC: O(n2)O(n^2)O(n2) — generates and compares nnn rotations of length nnn.

SC: O(n)O(n)O(n) — stores the current rotation and answer.

Algorithm / Flowchart

Start ↓ Initialize ans = s ↓ Generate each left rotation ↓ Is rotation < ans? ├── Yes → Update ans └── No → Keep ans ↓ More rotations? ├── Yes → Repeat └── No → Return ans

## 2. Better Solution — Candidate Elimination

Use two candidate starting positions, `i` and `j`, and compare their characters directly. When a mismatch occurs, eliminate the candidate that produces the larger character at that position.

Python

Run

```
class Solution:
    def lexiString(self, s: str) -> str:
        n = len(s)
        doubled = s + s

        i, j, k = 0, 1, 0

        while i < n and j < n and k < n:
            a = doubled[i + k]
            b = doubled[j + k]

            if a == b:
                k += 1
            elif a > b:
                i = i + k + 1
                if i <= j:
                    i = j + 1
                k = 0
            else:
                j = j + k + 1
                if j <= i:
                    j = i + 1
                k = 0

        start = min(i, j)
        return doubled[start:start + n]
```

TC: O(n)O(n)O(n)

SC: O(n)O(n)O(n) — doubled string and result.

Algorithm / Flowchart

Start ↓ Create doubled = s + s ↓ Initialize candidates i, j and match length k ↓ Compare doubled\(i+k\) and doubled\(j+k\) ├── Equal → Increment k ├── First greater → Eliminate candidate i └── Second greater → Eliminate candidate j ↓ Repeat until a candidate is eliminated or k = n ↓ Choose smaller remaining index ↓ Return rotation of length n

## 3. Optimal Solution — Booth's Algorithm

Booth's algorithm finds the lexicographically smallest rotation in linear time. It uses candidate starting positions and skips entire ranges of candidates after a mismatch.

Python

Run

```
class Solution:
    def lexiString(self, s: str) -> str:
        n = len(s)
        ss = s + s

        i, j, k = 0, 1, 0

        while i < n and j < n and k < n:
            a = ss[i + k]
            b = ss[j + k]

            if a == b:
                k += 1
                continue

            if a > b:
                i += k + 1
                if i <= j:
                    i = j + 1
            else:
                j += k + 1
                if j <= i:
                    j = i + 1

            k = 0

        start = min(i, j)
        return ss[start:start + n]
```

TC: O(n)O(n)O(n)

SC: O(n)O(n)O(n) — doubled string and returned result.

Algorithm / Flowchart

Start ↓ Create ss = s + s ↓ Set candidates i = 0, j = 1, k = 0 ↓ Compare characters at i+k and j+k ├── Equal → k += 1 ├── First greater → Skip candidates starting at i └── Second greater → Skip candidates starting at j ↓ Reset k after mismatch ↓ Continue while both candidates are valid ↓ start = min(i, j) ↓ Return ss\(start : start+n\) ↓ End

Note: The better and optimal solutions use the same linear-time candidate-elimination principle. Booth's algorithm is the standard name for this approach; both implementations run in O(n)O(n)O(n) time and O(n)O(n)O(n) auxiliary space for the doubled string.
